// Second witnesses to Lenain's text, read by hand where this edition needs
// one. The edition itself transcribes Google Books ZqgpxTZ43HkC, a later
// reissue (recorded as 1909) that was reset from scratch: its line breaks,
// page contents and spelling are not the 1823 printing's. These are the two
// other copies consulted, and what each prints where an editorial note turns
// on it. See plan 033, "Other witnesses".
//
// print1823 — the first printing, Amiens 1823: Koninklijke Bibliotheek copy,
//   digitised by Google Books as qt9SAAAAcAAJ (original paper wrapper, no
//   Papus preface). `at` is Lenain's printed page number:
//   https://books.google.com/books?id=qt9SAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA<at>
// ms5365 — Bibliothèque interuniversitaire de santé (Paris), Ms 5365: an
//   undated handwritten copy of the 1823 printing, without the first table.
//   archive.org BIUSante_ms05365, Licence ouverte (Etalab). `at` is the
//   archive.org leaf: https://archive.org/details/BIUSante_ms05365/page/n<at>
//   It is a copy, so where it differs from the 1823 printing that is the
//   copyist's reading, not evidence of what Lenain printed.
//
// `verdict` says whose slip a note's departure is:
//   reissue           the 1823 printing has what is required; the
//                     reissue's resetting introduced the error.
//   reissueChanged    the 1823 printing has another word, which the
//                     reissue replaced; not a misprint, but not Lenain's.
//   lenain            the 1823 printing has the same; the slip is Lenain's or
//                     his printer's, not the reissue's.
//   reissueCorrected  the 1823 printing has a slip the reissue does not.
//   agree             the 1823 printing agrees with this edition's reading.
//   absent            neither printing gives the value at all.
//   mixed             part of the value is each's; `remark` says which.
//
// Read 18 November 2026 from the page images, each by a reader not told the
// edition's own value, and the disagreements checked by eye. `reads` is the
// value only, in the edition's notation (degrees as from–to).
//
// The Hebrew names come in two sets. The twenty-five a person read from the
// reissue were looked up in both copies, as above. The forty-seven that two
// machine readings of the reissue agreed on were read afterwards, the same
// day, from the 1823 printing alone; no manuscript reading was taken for
// them. Every name was then compared with its triad of the Shem HaMephorash,
// derived from Exodus 14:19–21
// (packages/magick-data/src/kabbalah/shemHaMephorash.ts). Of the
// forty-seven, a reading is recorded here only where the 1823 printing or the
// verse differs from the reissue (12, 15, 24, 30, 33, 38, 51, 59, 64 and 69),
// or where the name does not end יה or אל (66, which ends אה in both
// printings). For 1, 4, 5, 7 and 8 the 1823 scan
// seems to show het where the reissue and the verse have he, but at the
// scan's resolution that cannot be settled, so nothing is recorded for them.
// The other thirty-one agree with the reissue, and their triads with the
// verse. In the apparatus, what the reissue prints for a name the machines
// read is its bare letters: the machines' marks were not trusted (see
// scripts/seventyTwoAngels/hebrew.ts), so the reissue's dots are recorded
// only for the names a person read.
//
// The nations and names of God (`people`, `godName`) are read where an entry
// departs from Kircher's plate, Lenain's source for them (kircherPlate.json5,
// beside this file), from the 1823 printing only, no manuscript. `reads` is
// the phrase as printed ("la langue des Samaritains"), or empty where the
// entry names none. They were read on 19 November 2026 from crops of the
// page images by a machine reader (Claude Sonnet 5.5) not told the edition's
// values or the plate's, and checked by eye against the page by another
// (Claude Opus 5.5); the two agreed on every one. Where the 1823 entry has
// what the reissue has, the departure is Lenain's (`lenain`); where it is
// silent too, `agree`.
